issue.body:
**Version(s) affected**: 3.7.3
**PHP version**: 7.3.33
**RabbitMQ version**: 4.1.4

**Description**  

[AMQPHeartbeatMissedException here](https://github.com/php-amqplib/php-amqplib/blob/8b72f1fb62b49f84c71a9df5bc32d12e432ffe98/PhpAmqpLib/Wire/IO/AbstractIO.php#L169) is never thrown when network connection between consumer and RabbitMQ server is lost. 


**How to reproduce**  
Start RabbitMQ on one host and run consumer code as below on another. Next disconnect the network cable linking the two machines.

```
#!/usr/bin/php -q
<?php
require('vendor/autoload.php');

use PhpAmqpLib\Connection\AMQPStreamConnection;
use PhpAmqpLib\Connection\Heartbeat\PCNTLHeartbeatSender;

use PhpAmqpLib\Message\AMQPMessage;

class Consumer
{
    private $reconnectTimeout = 500000;

    private $options = [
        'connection_timeout' => 3.0,
        'read_write_timeout' => 3.0,
        'keepalive' => false,
        'heartbeat' => 5
    ];

    /* @var AMQPStreamConnection */
    private $connection = null;

    /* @var PCNTLHeartbeatSender */
    private $heartbeatSender = null;

    public function run()
    {
        while (true) {
            try {
                // Connect to the AMQP service
                $this->connection = AMQPStreamConnection::create_connection(...., $this->options);

                $this->heartbeatSender = new PCNTLHeartbeatSender($this->connection);
                $this->heartbeatSender->register();

                // Start the consumer
                $this->consume();

            } catch(AMQPRuntimeException $e) {
                echo "AMQPRuntimeException exception: " . $e->getMessage(). PHP_EOL;
                $this->cleanupConnection();
                usleep($this->reconnectTimeout);
            } catch(\RuntimeException $e) {
                echo "Runtime exception: " . $e->getMessage(). PHP_EOL;
                $this->cleanupConnection();
                usleep($this->reconnectTimeout);
            } catch(\InvalidArgumentException $e) {
                echo "Invalid argument exception: " . $e->getMessage() . PHP_EOL;
                $this->cleanupConnection();
                usleep($this->reconnectTimeout);
            } catch(\ErrorException $e) {
                echo "Error exception: " . $e->getMessage() . PHP_EOL;
                $this->cleanupConnection();
                usleep($this->reconnectTimeout);
            } catch (\Exception $e) {
                echo "Exception exception: " . $e->getMessage(). PHP_EOL;
                usleep($this->reconnectTimeout);
            }
        }
    }

    protected function consume()
    {
        $channel = $this->connection->channel();

        $channel->queue_declare('test_queue', false, false, false, false);

        $channel->basic_consume('test_queue', '', false, true, false, false, [ $this, 'doWork' ]);

        while ($channel->is_consuming()) {
            $channel->wait();
        }
    }

    protected function cleanupConnection() {
        // Connection might already be closed.
        // Ignoring exceptions.
        try {
            if ($this->connection !== null) {
                $this->connection->close();
            }

            if ($this->heartbeatSender !== null) {
                $this->heartbeatSender->unregister();
            }
        } catch (\Exception $e) {
            // ignore the exception
        }
    }

    public function doWork($msg)
    {
        echo ' [x] Received ', $msg->body, "\n";
    }
}

$consumer = new Consumer();
$consumer->run();
```

The expected behavior is within 15 seconds we should see `AMQPHeartbeatMissedException` thrown. That however doesn't happen, because of the logic in [AbstractIO.php here:](https://github.com/php-amqplib/php-amqplib/blob/8b72f1fb62b49f84c71a9df5bc32d12e432ffe98/PhpAmqpLib/Wire/IO/AbstractIO.php#L144)

```
    protected function checkBrokerHeartbeat(): void
    {
        if ($this->heartbeat > 0 && ($this->last_read > 0 || $this->last_write > 0)) {
            // Below we are getting timestamp of last_read or last_write
            // even though we are not getting any response, as the network is disconnected
            // the condition:
            //   (($now - $lastActivity) > $this->heartbeat * 2 + 1)
            // is NOT met in the first iteration, so we proceed to sending another heartbeat
            // packet and updating last_write - even though the message will never reach RabbitMQ server !!!
            // This loop will continue indefinitely.

            $lastActivity = $this->getLastActivity();
            $now = microtime(true);
            if (($now - $lastActivity) > $this->heartbeat * 2 + 1) {
                $this->close();
                throw new AMQPHeartbeatMissedException('Missed server heartbeat');
            }
        }
    }
```

NOTE: it's **NOT POSSIBLE** to replicate this bug, by simply stopping `rabbit-server` process, as this will result in TCP connection "clean close" and `broken pipe` exception on PHP code end. The only way, is to unplug network cable, so the OS where `rabbit-server` and `PHP` code runs are not aware of the change in underlying networking being lost. When running on VMs, it's easy to replicate by setting network interfaces to "Disconnected" on the `rabbit-server` side. 


**Possible Solution**  
The solution is to change [getLastActivity method here](https://github.com/php-amqplib/php-amqplib/blob/8b72f1fb62b49f84c71a9df5bc32d12e432ffe98/PhpAmqpLib/Wire/IO/AbstractIO.php#L177C21-L177C36):
```
    /**
     * @return float|int
     */
    public function getLastActivity()
    {
        return max($this->last_read, $this->last_write);
    }
```

to:

```
    /**
     * @return float|int
     */
    public function getLastActivity()
    {
        return $this->last_read;
    }
```

as what really matters is if we did get a response from the server, not if we tried to send data to it (which OS will report as success, even though no data was in fact received on the other end of connection).

comment.body:
An automated reproduction of the same mechanism, plus data for the regression question.

Setup: php-amqplib 3.7.5, `AMQPStreamConnection` with heartbeat 15 s and read/write timeout 30 s, RabbitMQ 3.13.3 in Docker, PHP 8.4. Instead of cutting the network, I froze the broker with `docker pause`: TCP stays up, but nothing comes back.

| Client | Stock `getLastActivity()` | `getLastActivity()` returns `last_read`, as proposed here |
|---|---|---|
| Consumer: `basic_consume`, then `wait(null, true)` and `sleep(1)` in a loop. Broker frozen 5 s after connecting | No exception in 80 s | `AMQPHeartbeatMissedException` 31 s after connecting |
| Publisher: `basic_publish` once a second, never reads. Broker healthy | No exception in 50 s | `AMQPHeartbeatMissedException` 31 s after connecting |

So the change fixes the consumer and breaks a publish-only client. `StreamIO::write()` calls `checkBrokerHeartbeat()`, and a client that only publishes never reads, so `last_read` stays at the time of connecting.

On whether the broker still sends heartbeats: it does. After 30 s of publishing with the stock code and a healthy broker, 32 bytes were waiting in the socket: four heartbeat frames (`08 00 00 00 00 00 00 CE`) and nothing else. The client just never reads them.

One option, not tested: when `last_read` is too old, check without blocking whether the socket has unread data before throwing, and count unread data as activity from the broker.

The consumer case also affects Laravel's `rabbitmq:consume` (vyuldashev/laravel-queue-rabbitmq#673). There it runs in CI as experiment 2 of https://github.com/skybleak333/laravel-queue-lab.

<details><summary>Script for the table</summary>

```php
<?php
// php probe.php publisher|consumer <seconds>
// RabbitMQ on 127.0.0.1:5672. For "consumer", freeze the broker a few seconds after the start,
// e.g. with `docker pause <container>`. For the second column, change
// AbstractIO::getLastActivity() to return $this->last_read.
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use PhpAmqpLib\Connection\AMQPStreamConnection;
use PhpAmqpLib\Message\AMQPMessage;

[, $mode, $seconds] = $argv;
$start = microtime(true);

$connection = new AMQPStreamConnection('127.0.0.1', 5672, 'guest', 'guest', '/', false, 'AMQPLAIN', null, 'en_US', 3.0, 30.0, null, false, 15);
$channel = $connection->channel();
[$queue] = $channel->queue_declare('', false, false, true, true);

try {
    if ($mode === 'publisher') {
        while (microtime(true) - $start < $seconds) {
            $channel->basic_publish(new AMQPMessage('x'), '', $queue);
            sleep(1);
        }
    } else {
        $channel->basic_consume($queue, '', false, true, false, false, static function (): void {});

        while (microtime(true) - $start < $seconds) {
            $channel->wait(null, true);
            sleep(1);
        }
    }

    printf("%.1f s: no exception\n", microtime(true) - $start);
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    printf("%.1f s: %s: %s\n", microtime(true) - $start, get_class($e), $e->getMessage());
}
```

</details>

issue.body:
## Summary

When RabbitMQ closes a channel with `PRECONDITION_FAILED` (AMQP reply code 406), `pop()` re-throws the `AMQPProtocolChannelException` without wrapping it as a "Lost connection" exception. Laravel's `DetectsLostConnections::causedByLostConnection()` then fails to match the message, the worker swallows the error and keeps looping, **the PHP process stays alive but no longer consumes any messages on that channel**.

The pod stays `Ready=true` from Kubernetes' POV. Queue depth grows until something external notices.

This matches the symptoms described in #630 but the root cause and a precise fix have not been identified there. Opening a dedicated issue so the bug is searchable.

## Reproduction

Most common trigger in production: RabbitMQ's `consumer_timeout` setting (default 30 min since RMQ 3.12). A long-running job that doesn't ack within `consumer_timeout` causes the broker to send to the consumer:

```
Channel error on connection <0.xxxx.0> (host:port -> host:port, vhost: '...', user: '...'), channel 1:
operation none caused a channel exception precondition_failed:
delivery acknowledgement on channel 1 timed out. Timeout value used: 1800000 ms.
```

After this, the worker continues looping in `Worker::daemon()` but every subsequent `basic_get` call either errors or returns null. The PHP process remains alive (passes `ps`-grep liveness probes), but no messages are consumed.

Reproduced against:
- `vladimir-yuldashev/laravel-queue-rabbitmq` v14.1.0 (also confirmed in v14.4.0 / master at `63e5c375`)
- `php-amqplib/php-amqplib` 3.x
- RabbitMQ 3.13.3
- Laravel 11.x with `php artisan rabbitmq:consume` and `queue:work` (both affected)

## Root cause

In [`src/Queue/RabbitMQQueue.php`](https://github.com/vyuldashev/laravel-queue-rabbitmq/blob/63e5c375fa383cd3778a32c7ccf84b98764613c8/src/Queue/RabbitMQQueue.php#L257-L295) `pop()` (line 257 on master at `63e5c375`):

```php
} catch (AMQPProtocolChannelException $exception) {
    if ($exception->amqp_reply_code === 404) {
        $this->getChannel(true);
        return null;
    }
    throw $exception;                          // ← code 406 falls through here
} catch (AMQPChannelClosedException|AMQPConnectionClosedException $exception) {
    throw new AMQPRuntimeException(
        'Lost connection: '.$exception->getMessage(), ...
    );                                          // ← only THIS branch makes the worker restart
}
```

- 404 (`NOT_FOUND`) is handled — channel is reopened, `null` returned, loop continues. Correct.
- 406 (`PRECONDITION_FAILED` — most commonly the ack timeout, but also other broker-enforced preconditions) falls through to `throw $exception`.
- The `AMQPProtocolChannelException` message — `"PRECONDITION_FAILED - delivery acknowledgement on channel 1 timed out. ..."` — does not contain `"Lost connection"`, so `Illuminate\Database\DetectsLostConnections::causedByLostConnection()` returns false.
- Laravel's `Worker::runJob()`/`process()` catches the exception, reports it, then the worker loop continues. The dead channel is never reopened.

After 406 the channel is gone — the next `basic_get($queue)` on the same channel will throw `AMQPChannelClosedException` and *that* would be wrapped correctly — but the channel is reset on every `getChannel(true)` call only inside the 404 branch. There is no recovery path for 406.

## Impact

Production outage shape we hit:
- Producer keeps publishing — queue depth grows.
- Consumer pod `Ready=true`, PHP `queue:work` process alive, no liveness check at the broker level.
- Eventually noticed by external alerting on queue depth (`QueueConsumeStop` / `NotEnoughWorkers` style alerts).
- Mitigation: manual `kubectl rollout restart` of the consumer deployment.

## Proposed fix

Wrap 406 the same way 404 (handled) and `AMQPChannelClosedException` (already wrapped) are handled — surface it as a "Lost connection" exception so the worker tears down cleanly and the supervisor (k8s `restartPolicy: Always`, supervisord, Horizon, etc.) brings it back with a fresh channel.

```diff
 } catch (AMQPProtocolChannelException $exception) {
     if ($exception->amqp_reply_code === 404) {
         $this->getChannel(true);
         return null;
     }
+
+    // PRECONDITION_FAILED (406) means the broker closed the channel and we
+    // cannot continue on it. The most common trigger is the consumer ack
+    // timeout (RabbitMQ `consumer_timeout` setting). Surface this as a
+    // "Lost connection" exception so DetectsLostConnections triggers a
+    // clean worker restart instead of leaving the worker alive with a
+    // dead channel.
+    if ($exception->amqp_reply_code === 406) {
+        throw new AMQPRuntimeException(
+            'Lost connection: '.$exception->getMessage(),
+            $exception->getCode(),
+            $exception
+        );
+    }
+
     throw $exception;
 }
```

Happy to open a PR with this change + a unit test asserting the wrapping for both 404 (returns null) and 406 (throws `AMQPRuntimeException` with `"Lost connection: "` prefix). LMK if a different approach is preferred — e.g. wrapping *every* non-404 protocol channel exception, given that any broker-closed channel is unrecoverable on this connection.

## Workaround (current)

Subclass `RabbitMQQueue` and override `pop()` to wrap 406 manually:

```php
class RabbitMQQueue extends BaseRabbitMQQueue
{
    public function pop($queue = null)
    {
        try {
            return parent::pop($queue);
        } catch (AMQPProtocolChannelException $e) {
            if ($e->amqp_reply_code === 406) {
                throw new AMQPRuntimeException(
                    'Lost connection: '.$e->getMessage(), $e->getCode(), $e
                );
            }
            throw $e;
        }
    }
}
```

Register via `'worker' => App\Queue\RabbitMQQueue::class` in the connection config.

## Related

- #630 — reporter pasted the exact `PRECONDITION_FAILED - delivery acknowledgement on channel 1 timed out` error, conversation went toward `--max-time` / `keepalive` workarounds but never identified this code path.
- RabbitMQ `consumer_timeout` docs: https://www.rabbitmq.com/consumers.html#acknowledgement-timeout
- Laravel `DetectsLostConnections`: https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/11.x/src/Illuminate/Database/DetectsLostConnections.php
